[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Fig. 1 | Overview of framework. a . We curated a diverse collection of light microscopy datasets.  The  left  panel  summarizes  dataset  diversity  across  microscopy  types,  cell types,  perturbation  conditions  and  species.  The  right  panel  presents  a  hierarchical organization  of  the  datasets,  grouped  into  pre-training  and  evaluation  sets,  with  the number  of  images  annotated  at  each  level  of  the  hierarchy.  Microscopy  types  are", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with three labeled panels: `a. Dataset characterization`, `b. Model architecture`, and `c. Downstream analysis based on learned image embedding`.\n\nPanel `a` summarizes dataset characterization. A circular chart shows microscopy/data categories including `BF`, `DIC`, `Conf`, `WF`, `SR`, `QPI`, `PC`, `Cell type (100+)`, `Perturbation (300+)`, and species labels including `Human`, `Mouse`, `Yeast`, and `Bacteria`. The center contains example microscopy cell images. A right-side hierarchy organizes data by `Granularity`, `Scale`, `Attribute`, and `Number`, mapping `Single-cell`, `Full-field`, `Subcellular`, `Cell-level`, and `Population` to attributes such as `Protein localization`, `Cell identity/state`, and `Perturbation response`. Counts are shown for `Pre-training` and `Evaluation`, including values such as `72,832`, `2,622,150`, `40,000`, `156,669`, `95,125`, and `117,628`.\n\nPanel `b` shows a model architecture for single-cell image-text learning. Inputs include a generated `Caption` with keywords such as `Species`, `Source`, `Microscopy`, `Cell type`, and `Biological attribute`, plus a `Single-cell image`. The caption is passed through a `Tokenizer` into `Text tokens`. The image is passed through an `Image encoder` into `Image tokens`. A `Q-Former` module connects text/image tokens to image-text alignment, represented by a matrix comparing `Image feature` entries `I1 ... Im` with `Text feature` entries `T1 ... Tn`. A second shared-weight `Q-Former` receives masked image tokens, followed by an `Image decoder`, performing `Mask tokens reconstruction`.\n\nPanel `c` illustrates downstream analysis using learned image embeddings. `Evaluation images` are input into `scMIR`, producing a learned image embedding matrix. The embeddings are used for `Cell classification`, `Morphological inference`, `Cell clustering`, and `Batch correction`, shown as schematic point clusters and transformed groupings.\n\nVisible biological source objects include single-cell microscopy images and schematic cells. The figure describes transformations from microscopy images and captions into tokenized text/image representations, learned embeddings, reconstructed image tokens, and downstream biological analysis outputs."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 2 | Classification and clustering performance, visualization of scMIR embeddings. a . Radar plot of F1-score performance (scaled ×100) across 17 classification tasks from 13 light microscopy  datasets. scMIR  is compared  with  task-oriented models, CytoImageNet,  EfficientNetB0,  and  Microsnoop. b .  Quantitative  assessment  of unsupervised  cross-dataset  alignment  using  normalized  mutual  information  (NMI), purity score (PS), and adjusted Rand index (ARI) computed from joint embeddings (metrics scaled ×100). c . t-SNE visualization of scMIR embeddings. Left, embeddings colored  by  dataset  identity  demonstrate  compact  dataset-specific  clustering  despite experimental  heterogeneity.  Right,  the  same  embeddings  colored  by  dataset-level biological attributes reveal coherent higher-level biological organization across studies and microscopy modalities. Source data are provided as a Source Data file.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about scMIR performance and embeddings.\n\nPanel a: “Classification performance”\nA radar/spider chart comparing classification performance across multiple microscopy image datasets. Dataset labels around the chart include BBB-C048, BBB-C014, CNMC, Raabin, DIBaS, CoNSeP, LiveCell, FCD, AINU, COOS7-5, COOS7-4, COOS7-3, COOS7-2, COOS7-1, CYCLOPs, CM4AI, and AIRFIHA. Methods shown in the legend are Task-oriented, CytolImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR. The red scMIR curve generally forms the outer envelope, indicating stronger classification performance across most datasets.\n\nPanel b: “Clustering performance”\nA grouped bar chart comparing CytolImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR on clustering metrics NMI, PS, and ARI. scMIR has the highest visible values: NMI 70.4, PS 56.5, and ARI 36.7. Other methods are lower, with approximate values shown above bars, e.g. NMI 63.4, 66.3, 68.4; PS 47.2, 48.7, 52.0; ARI 29.5, 29.4, 33.8.\n\nPanel c: “Visualization of scMIR embeddings”\nTwo t-SNE scatter plots of scMIR embeddings.\n\nLeft plot: “Visualization of dataset”\nPoints are colored by dataset/source. The legend includes AINU, AIRFIHA, BBBC014, BBBC048, CM4AI, CNMC, COOS7, CYCLOPs, CoNSeP, DIBaS, FCD, LIVECell, and Raabin. The plot shows separated and partially overlapping clusters in t-SNE space.\n\nRight plot: “Visualization of dataset attribute”\nThe same embedding space colored by biological or dataset attribute categories: Bacteria, Cancer, Immune, Immune Cancer, Somatic Stem, and Subcellular. An arrow labels a cluster as “CNMC,” and another annotation indicates “CD4/CD8.” The visible finding is that scMIR embeddings group samples by both dataset identity and broader biological/image attributes.\n\nBiological source objects visible or implied by labels include microscopy-derived cell, tissue, and biomedical image datasets, including bacteria, cancer, immune-cell, stem-cell, and subcellular image sources. The main transformation shown is embedding visualization using t-SNE. The model/interface being evaluated is scMIR against baseline image representation models."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Fig. 3 | Perturbation phenotypic profiling of scMIR embeddings. a . Schematic overview of phenotypic inference from perturbation image embeddings. Chemical perturbations", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about image-based profiling of chemical perturbations.\n\nPanel a shows a workflow: a multi-well plate is imaged by microscopy, producing cellular fluorescence images. These images are passed through a “Feature extractor” model/interface to generate “Image embeddings.” Embeddings from wells of chemical perturbation are then compared, with dashed circles indicating wells associated with the same chemical perturbation.\n\nPanel b is titled “Performance comparison on BBBC021” and contains three scatter plots comparing methods by Mean Average Precision (mAP) on the x-axis and Folds of Enrichment on the y-axis. The subplots are:\n- “Treatment level-MoAs”\n- “Well level-Compounds”\n- “Well level-MoAs”\n\nMethods in the legend are CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR. scMIR appears as the red point and has the highest performance in the shown plots.\n\nPanel c is titled “Treatment representations clustering” for BBBC021. It shows a UMAP scatter plot with axes UMAP1 and UMAP2. Points represent control wells, well-level profiles, and treatment-level profiles. Labeled biological/chemical mechanism clusters include Epithelial, Protein synthesis, Actin disruptors, DNA damage, Kinase inhibitors, and Microtubule destabilizers.\n\nPanel d is titled “Heatmap visualization of of AZ-A perturbation representations” for BBBC021. It shows a heatmap with DMSO and multiple dose/replicate-like row labels on the left, and a color scale from about -3 to +3 on the right. The heatmap visualizes learned perturbation representation values or embedding features across conditions."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 9, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single heatmap panel showing many rows of features/samples against labeled columns of biological taxa. The columns include an initial `DNSO` label followed by microbial genus/species-style labels such as `A2 (4)`, `anisomycin`, `desmococcus`, `emetic`, `equitabilis`, `episodica`, `flavumaurantiacum`, `minorygin`, `nocobacta`, `taylor`, and `viridimicis` variants with replicate/group numbers in parentheses.\n\nVisible elements:\n- One rectangular clustered-style heatmap, though no dendrograms are visible.\n- X-axis labels are rotated vertically and densely packed.\n- No visible y-axis labels.\n- A vertical color bar at right ranges approximately from `-4` in blue through `0` white to `4` red.\n- Color encoding appears to represent normalized abundance/expression/intensity values, with red indicating higher values and blue indicating lower values.\n- The heatmap contains block-like patterns of red and blue across groups, suggesting differential signal across taxa/conditions.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Likely microbial taxa or biological sample groups, based on genus/species-like labels.\n- No cells, tissues, organisms, microscopy images, or molecular structures are directly visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data appear transformed into a scaled matrix or z-score-like heatmap due to the centered diverging color scale.\n- No model architecture, workflow diagram, or interface is shown.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- Several column groups show localized enrichment or depletion patterns.\n- Strong red regions are visible near labels including `equitabilis`, `taylor`, and some `nocobacta`/related columns.\n- Strong blue regions appear in multiple blocks, including early `A2/AZ` columns and some mid-to-late taxa groups.\n- Exact quantitative findings cannot be determined beyond the color-scale patterns because row labels and caption are absent."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Fig.  4 | a .  Schematic  illustration  of  hierarchical  feature  aggregation  on  the  Human Protein Atlas  (HPA)  dataset.  Single-cell  image  embeddings  are  first  extracted  from individual microscopy images and then aggregated to obtain higher-level representations at multiple biological scales, including cell line-level, protein localization-level, and protein-level features. b . Similarity matrix of protein localization groups computed from scMIR-derived protein-level features, with expertdefined  hierarchical  organelle  annotations  indicated  (red  and  yellow). c .  Cell-line similarity  matrices  derived  from  bulk  mRNA  expression  (left)  and  morphology-", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure about scMIR embeddings from HPA single-cell images.\n\nPanel a: “Feature aggregation on HPA images”\n- Shows multiple single-cell fluorescence microscopy images as biological source objects.\n- Pipeline diagram: single-cell images pass through a “Feature extractor,” producing feature vectors.\n- Features are combined by “Feature aggregation.”\n- Outputs are three embedding types: “Cell line embeddings,” “Protein localization embeddings,” and “Protein embeddings.”\n- Model/interface elements are schematic blocks and arrows, not code or UI.\n\nPanel b: “scMIR protein localization embedding similarity heatmap”\n- Heatmap of protein localization embedding similarity.\n- Rows/columns are subcellular localization labels including nucleoli rim, nucleoli, nucleoli fibrillar center, vesicles, peroxisomes, lysosomes, endosomes, lipid droplets, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, cytoplasmic bodies, cytosol, centriolar satellite, centrosome, microtubule ends, microtubules, intermediate filaments, actin filaments, focal adhesion sites, cell junctions, plasma membrane, mitochondria, nuclear membrane, nucleoplasm, nuclear bodies, and nuclear speckles.\n- Red outlined regions annotate localization groups: Nucleoli, Endomembrane system, Cytosol, Cytoskeleton, Plasma membrane, Mitochondria, and Nucleus.\n- Darker blue blocks indicate higher similarity within some localization groups.\n\nPanel c: “Cell line similarity heatmaps based on mRNA and scMIR embeddings”\n- Two side-by-side heatmaps compare cell line similarity.\n- Left heatmap labeled “mRNA.”\n- Right heatmap labeled “scMIR embeddings.”\n- Cell lines listed include NB4, SK-MEL-30, MCF-7, Hep-G2, A-549, OE19, HaCaT, SuSa, PC-3, HeLa, CACO-2, EFO-21, HDLM-2, U-251MG, A-431, SiHa, GAMG, U2OS, BJ, hTCEpi, RPTEC/TERT1, HUVEC/TERT2, HCN-M2, ASC52telo, hTERT-RPE1, AF22, RT-4, RH30, HEK293, REH, HAP1, SH-SY5Y, THP-1, JURKAT, HEL, and K-562.\n- Color bar shows cosine similarity from -1.00 to 1.00.\n\nPanel d: “Mantel statistic comparison”\n- Bar chart comparing Mantel statistic for “Cell line” and “Protein localization.”\n- Methods shown by color: CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.\n- scMIR has the highest visible values: 0.29 for Cell line and 0.31 for Protein localization.\n- Other labeled values: Cell line 0.22, 0.20, 0.24; Protein localization 0.14, 0.16, 0.17.\n\nPanel e: “Comparison of protein interaction prediction performance”\n- Bar chart comparing methods: CytImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.\n- Metrics: Spearman, Kendall, and TopBottomSep@10%.\n- scMIR has the highest visible performance on all metrics: Spearman 4.69, Kendall 3.14, TopBottomSep@10% 2.70.\n- Other visible values: Spearman 0.96, 1.14, 2.58; Kendall 0.64, 0.77, 1.72; TopBottomSep@10% 0.36, 0.44, 0.66.\n\nOverall finding shown: scMIR embeddings derived from HPA single-cell images preserve biologically meaningful subcellular localization and cell-line similarity structure and outperform the comparison image embedding methods in the plotted protein interaction prediction metrics."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 13, "caption": "Fig. 5 | Batch effect of image embeddings. UMAP visualizations of image-level features extracted  by  scMIR  and  representative  pretrained  baselines  on  the  COOS7  [53], cpg0000 [1] and BBBC021 [58] datasets. Features are colored by plate IDs. IMAD quantifies  the  consistency  of  UMAP  patterns  across  batches,  with  higher  values indicating improved batch-effect mitigation. UMAP: Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection; IMAD: inverse median absolute deviation.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure comparing UMAP embeddings across datasets and feature/model methods.\n\nVisible structure:\n- A 3 row x 4 column grid of scatter/UMAP plots.\n- Rows are labeled vertically by dataset:\n  - `CODS7`\n  - `cpg0000`\n  - `BBBC021`\n- Columns compare model or feature sources:\n  - `CytoImageNet`\n  - `EfficientNetB0`\n  - `Microsnoop`\n  - `scMIR`\n- Each panel title reports an `IMAD` score.\n  - For `CODS7`: CytoImageNet `IMAD=0.75`, EfficientNetB0 `IMAD=1.03`, Microsnoop `IMAD=1.15`, scMIR `IMAD=1.47`\n  - For `cpg0000`: CytoImageNet `IMAD=0.415`, EfficientNetB0 `IMAD=0.352`, Microsnoop `IMAD=0.252`, scMIR `IMAD=0.421`\n  - For `BBBC021`: CytoImageNet `IMAD=0.493`, EfficientNetB0 `IMAD=0.518`, Microsnoop `IMAD=0.369`, scMIR `IMAD=0.608`\n\nAxes and visual encodings:\n- Each panel has axes labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`.\n- Points are colored by `Plates IDs`, with a legend of multiple colored dots at the bottom.\n- Marginal density curves appear above and to the right of each UMAP scatter plot, showing distributions along UMAP1 and UMAP2 for the colored groups.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The figure appears to analyze microscopy or cell-image-derived datasets, including `CytoImageNet`, `cpg0000`, and `BBBC021`.\n- The plotted objects are embedded biological image samples or cell/microscopy-derived feature representations, colored by plate identity.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Feature representations from different models or methods are projected into 2D using UMAP.\n- Compared representations include CytoImageNet, EfficientNetB0, Microsnoop, and scMIR.\n- IMAD is used as a quantitative comparison metric for each embedding.\n\nVisible findings:\n- `scMIR` has the highest reported IMAD in the `CODS7` and `BBBC021` rows.\n- `Microsnoop` has the lowest reported IMAD in the `cpg0000` and `BBBC021` rows.\n- The embeddings show varying degrees of clustering and plate-associated separation across methods and datasets."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 38, "caption": "Fig. S1 | t-SNE visualization of CytoImageNet embeddings. Embeddings colored by dataset identity.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single t-SNE scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset.” The x-axis is labeled “t-SNE 1” and the y-axis is labeled “t-SNE 2.” Points are colored by dataset, with a legend titled “Dataset” listing AINU, AIRFIHA, BBBC014, BBBC048, CM4AI, CNMC, COOS-7, CYCLOPs, CoNSeP, DIBaS, FCD, LIVECell, and Raabin.\n\nThe figure visualizes embedded samples as colored point clusters. Several datasets form distinct separated clusters, while some appear as multiple subclusters. No biological source objects are directly shown; the plot likely represents transformed image/sample features projected into two dimensions using t-SNE. No model architecture or interface is visible. The main visible finding is dataset-level clustering/separation in the embedding space."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 39, "caption": "Fig. S2 | t-SNE visualization of CytoImageNet embeddings. Embeddings colored by dataset-level biological attributes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a 2D t-SNE scatter plot titled “Visualization of dataset attribute.” It has one visible panel with axes labeled “t-SNE 1” and “t-SNE 2.”\n\nThe points are colored by dataset attribute, with a legend on the right labeled “Dataset attribute.” Legend categories include:\n\n- Bacteria: blue\n- Cancer: green\n- Immune: purple\n- Immune Cancer: pink\n- Somatic Stem: yellow-green\n- Subcellular: cyan\n\nThe plot shows multip